\details{
\strong{All three columns are natural logarithms.} Equations (3) and (4) are
written in logs: the error correction term is a log gap and the first
differences are growth rates. \code{\link[=recm]{recm()}} takes bare column names and cannot
apply \code{log()} inline, so the transform is applied here rather than being
pushed onto every caller.

Equation (3), the frictionless level, imposes the permanent income
restriction that the weights on human and non-human wealth sum to one:

\deqn{\log c^{f}_t = \kappa + (1 - 0.282) \log HW_t + 0.282 \log NHW_t}

Equation (4), which this dataset is shaped for, is the rational error
correction specification at adjustment cost order \eqn{m = 2}:

\deqn{\Delta c_t = -0.15 (c_{t-1} - c^{f}_{t-1}) + 0.08 \Delta c_{t-1}
  + 0.78 \sum_{i=1}^{20} E[\Delta c^{f}_{t+i}] + 0.14 \Delta Yd_t
  + 0.01 \Delta CCONF_t + \varepsilon_t}

Consumer confidence is deliberately not included in this dataset, so the
\eqn{\Delta CCONF} term cannot be reproduced. Note that
\eqn{-0.15(c_{t-1} - c^{f}_{t-1})} is \eqn{+0.15(c^{f}_{t-1} - c_{t-1})},
which is \code{\link[=recm]{recm()}}'s own sign convention.
}
\section{How this differs from LENS}{

Three departures matter, and none of them is cosmetic.

\strong{Human wealth.} In LENS this is the expected discounted sum of
future real disposable income flows, generated by projecting income growth
40 quarters out with ToTEM. ToTEM is not public, so here the projection
comes from an autoregression in the growth of real per capita disposable
income, fitted over the whole sample and iterated forward 40 quarters from
each date, discounted at 0.75 per cent a quarter. This makes human wealth
close to a scaled level of current income, which is precisely what the
ToTEM projection is not.

\strong{Non-human wealth.} LENS defines this as financial plus real estate
net wealth at market value. Here it is the residential plus non-residential
capital stock (tables 36-10-0099 and 36-10-0098, geometric end-year net
stock at current prices). Those are published annually, so they are
interpolated to quarterly against an indicator accumulated from quarterly
investment, exactly reproducing the published figure in the fourth quarter
of each year and splicing forward past the last annual benchmark.

\strong{Nominal against real.} \code{cnds_pc} is nominal while human wealth is
real, so the error correction gap carries the price level. This is a
deliberate choice, not an oversight.
}

\section{Reproducing the published estimates}{

It does not. Estimating equation (4) on this dataset gives a speed of
adjustment near zero against the published 0.15, and a negative coefficient
on real disposable income growth against the published \eqn{+0.14}.

This was checked across every combination of a nominal or deflated
dependent variable, capital stock or National Balance Sheet Accounts net
worth as non-human wealth, and aggregate or per capita disposable income.
The speed of adjustment stayed between 0.000 and 0.008 in all eight, and
the income coefficient stayed negative in all eight, so none of those three
choices is the cause.

The binding constraint is the human wealth proxy. Because it is close to a
scaled current income, the forward term \eqn{Z_t} already loads heavily on
income growth, which both leaves the error correction gap close to a unit
root (the first order autocorrelation of the gap is 0.99 as shipped, 0.95
with balance sheet net worth) and makes the separate \eqn{\Delta Yd} term
collinear with \eqn{Z_t}, which is the likely source of its negative sign.

Treat this as a worked example of assembling a LENS-shaped dataset from
public data, not as a replication of the published equation. Reproducing it
would need the ToTEM-based human wealth series.
}

